 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** a `ContextVar` plus werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects. This is the same design as Flask's.\n\n**`src/tessel/globals.py`**\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` named `\"tessel.app_ctx\"`.\n- Each name is a `werkzeug.local.LocalProxy` over `_app_var`. On every access, the proxy reads the context variable's current value and forwards the operation to the attribute it names:\n  - `app_ctx` (line 41) is the `AppContext` itself.\n  - `active_app` (line 44) is `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` app.\n  - `g` (line 47) is `AppContext.g`, the `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n  - `request` (line 57) is `AppContext.request`, a `Request` from `wrappers.py`.\n  - `session` (line 60) is `AppContext.session`.\n- If nothing is bound, the proxy raises a \"Working outside of application context\" or \"outside of request context\" error. `request` and `session` use the request message.\n\n**`src/tessel/ctx.py`**\n- `AppContext` binds itself on push with `_app_var.set(self)` (line 433). On pop it calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` (line 498).\n- Because it is a `ContextVar`, each thread or async task sees its own value. Nested contexts restore the previous one.\n\nRequest data is stored on the same `AppContext`. The old separate `request_ctx` has been merged into `app_ctx`. The `request_ctx` name in `globals.py` now only returns `app_ctx`, with a deprecation warning.",
